# Offline eval cases for Eris. One JSON object per line; blank lines and '#' comments are skipped.
# Each case supplies its own fake search results and page bodies, so `eris eval` never touches
# the network. Every case includes at least one distractor source that is topically adjacent but
# does not answer the question -- that is what makes hit-rate@k a real measurement rather than a
# formality.
{"question": "What did Python 3.13 change about the global interpreter lock?", "expected_url": "https://docs.example.org/python/3.13/whatsnew", "note": "target buried among release-note sections; distractors are adjacent Python topics", "sources": [{"url": "https://docs.example.org/python/3.13/whatsnew", "title": "What's New In Python 3.13", "snippet": "Free-threaded CPython and the experimental JIT.", "text": "What's New In Python 3.13.\n\nInstallation and packaging notes. The installer now offers an optional build variant on Windows and macOS. Downstream redistributors should read the build configuration notes before packaging.\n\nFree-threaded CPython. CPython 3.13 ships an experimental free-threaded build in which the global interpreter lock is disabled. The free-threaded build is selected at compile time with the --disable-gil configure flag, and it is distributed as a separate binary rather than as the default interpreter. Because the global interpreter lock is disabled, threads can execute Python bytecode in parallel on multiple cores, but C extensions must be rebuilt and audited for thread safety before they can be loaded. Single-threaded performance in the free-threaded build is measurably slower than the default build.\n\nA new interactive interpreter. The REPL now supports multi-line editing, colourised tracebacks, and interactive help paging.\n\nAn experimental just-in-time compiler. A copy-and-patch JIT is included behind a build flag and is disabled by default.\n\nDeprecations. Several long-deprecated modules were removed from the standard library in this release."}, {"url": "https://blog.example.com/python-async-patterns", "title": "Async patterns in modern Python", "snippet": "A tour of asyncio task groups.", "text": "Async patterns in modern Python. Task groups give structured concurrency to asyncio code, so a failure in one child task cancels its siblings deterministically. This article walks through migrating callback-based code to coroutines, choosing between threads and coroutines for I/O-bound work, and measuring event-loop lag under load. Threading is discussed only as a comparison point for I/O-bound workloads; the interpreter internals are out of scope."}, {"url": "https://wiki.example.net/cpython-history", "title": "A short history of CPython releases", "snippet": "Release cadence from 2.7 to today.", "text": "A short history of CPython releases. CPython moved to an annual release cadence with version 3.9, shortening the window between feature releases and shifting long-term maintenance onto security-only branches. This page lists release managers, release dates, and end-of-life dates for each minor version. It does not cover the contents of individual releases."}]}
{"question": "How much did the Voyager 1 spacecraft weigh at launch?", "expected_url": "https://space.example.gov/voyager/factsheet", "note": "numeric fact; distractor covers the same mission but not the mass", "sources": [{"url": "https://space.example.gov/voyager/factsheet", "title": "Voyager 1 fact sheet", "snippet": "Mission parameters and spacecraft mass.", "text": "Voyager 1 fact sheet.\n\nLaunch. Voyager 1 launched on 5 September 1977 aboard a Titan IIIE-Centaur from Cape Canaveral.\n\nSpacecraft mass. The spacecraft had a launch mass of 825.5 kilograms, of which roughly 105 kilograms was the science instrument payload. Propellant accounted for a further 100 kilograms at launch.\n\nPower. Three radioisotope thermoelectric generators supplied 470 watts at launch, declining by roughly four watts per year.\n\nInstruments. Eleven instruments flew, including imaging science, infrared and ultraviolet spectrometers, and a magnetometer on a thirteen-metre boom."}, {"url": "https://news.example.com/voyager-interstellar", "title": "Voyager 1 reaches interstellar space", "snippet": "Crossing the heliopause.", "text": "Voyager 1 reaches interstellar space. Instrument data indicated that the spacecraft crossed the heliopause in August 2012, becoming the first human-made object to enter the interstellar medium. Plasma density measurements were the decisive evidence, since the onboard plasma science instrument had failed years earlier. The article covers the crossing, the signal delay, and the power budget that limits how long the mission can continue. It gives no launch-configuration figures."}]}
{"question": "What is the maximum request payload size for the service API?", "expected_url": "https://api.example.io/docs/limits", "note": "the answer lives in a quotas table alongside unrelated limits", "sources": [{"url": "https://api.example.io/docs/quickstart", "title": "API quickstart", "snippet": "Authenticate and send your first request.", "text": "API quickstart. Create a key in the console, then send a signed request to the v2 endpoint. This guide covers authentication headers, the SDK install command, pagination cursors, and error retry semantics. Quotas and payload limits are documented separately under Limits."}, {"url": "https://api.example.io/docs/limits", "title": "Service limits and quotas", "snippet": "Rate limits, payload sizes, and concurrency caps.", "text": "Service limits and quotas.\n\nRate limits. The default rate limit is 1,000 requests per minute per key, burstable to 2,000 for up to ten seconds.\n\nRequest payload size. The maximum request payload size is 10 MiB per request. Requests exceeding this limit are rejected with HTTP 413 before the body is read. Clients uploading larger objects must use the multipart upload endpoint, which accepts parts of up to 5 GiB each.\n\nResponse size. Responses are capped at 6 MiB; larger result sets must be paginated.\n\nConcurrency. Each account may hold 50 concurrent long-lived connections."}, {"url": "https://status.example.io/incidents/2024", "title": "Incident history", "snippet": "Past outages and postmortems.", "text": "Incident history. This page lists incidents affecting the API, with start and end times, affected regions, and links to postmortems. Elevated error rates on the write path are the most common category. No configuration limits or quotas are described here."}]}
{"question": "Which cities host the organisation's primary data centres?", "expected_url": "https://corp.example.com/infrastructure/regions", "note": "list-style fact spread across a long page", "sources": [{"url": "https://corp.example.com/about", "title": "About the organisation", "snippet": "History, mission and leadership.", "text": "About the organisation. Founded in 2004, the company operates in eleven countries and employs roughly 4,000 people. This page covers the founding story, the executive team, the annual report archive, and the careers portal. Facility locations are listed under Infrastructure."}, {"url": "https://corp.example.com/infrastructure/regions", "title": "Infrastructure regions", "snippet": "Data centre footprint and availability zones.", "text": "Infrastructure regions.\n\nOverview. The platform runs from primary data centres in Dublin, Singapore, and Portland, each with three independent availability zones and dual-carrier transit.\n\nEdge presence. Twenty-two edge sites terminate TLS closer to users; these are caching points of presence and do not hold customer data at rest.\n\nRedundancy. Every primary region replicates asynchronously to one other primary region, and Dublin and Portland form the default disaster-recovery pair.\n\nCompliance. Regional data-residency commitments are documented per jurisdiction in the trust centre."}]}
{"question": "What caused the 2024 service outage on the write path?", "expected_url": "https://status.example.io/postmortem/2024-write-path", "note": "causal question; a runbook distractor shares heavy vocabulary with the answer", "sources": [{"url": "https://status.example.io/postmortem/2024-write-path", "title": "Postmortem: write path outage", "snippet": "Root cause and remediation.", "text": "Postmortem: write path outage.\n\nSummary. Write requests failed or timed out for 47 minutes across two regions.\n\nRoot cause. A schema migration added a non-null column without a default to the primary write table. The migration acquired an exclusive lock for the duration of the table rewrite, and because the rewrite ran inside a single transaction on a table of 900 million rows, every concurrent write blocked behind the lock until the statement timeout fired. Connection pools saturated within ninety seconds and the resulting retry storm extended the impact after the lock was released.\n\nDetection. Automated alerting fired on write error rate within two minutes.\n\nRemediation. The migration was rolled back. Future column additions must be nullable, backfilled in batches, and gated behind a lock-timeout guard.\n\nAction items. Add lock-timeout defaults to the migration tooling and add a pre-merge check that rejects blocking DDL."}, {"url": "https://status.example.io/runbooks/write-path", "title": "Write path runbook", "snippet": "On-call procedures for write failures.", "text": "Write path runbook. When write error rate exceeds one percent, check connection pool saturation, replica lag, and any migration currently in flight. Escalate to the storage on-call if lock waits are visible in the database. This runbook documents procedure only and records no specific incident, root cause, or timeline."}]}
{"question": "What is the recommended way to rotate the signing key?", "expected_url": "https://docs.example.io/security/key-rotation", "note": "procedural question; distractor is adjacent security documentation", "sources": [{"url": "https://docs.example.io/security/overview", "title": "Security overview", "snippet": "Threat model and controls.", "text": "Security overview. This page describes the threat model, the encryption-at-rest configuration, the audit logging pipeline, and the responsible disclosure process. Operational key procedures are documented separately."}, {"url": "https://docs.example.io/security/key-rotation", "title": "Rotating signing keys", "snippet": "Overlapping key rotation without downtime.", "text": "Rotating signing keys.\n\nRecommended procedure. Rotate with overlapping validity rather than a hard cutover. Publish the new public key to the JWKS endpoint first and wait at least one full token lifetime so verifiers can cache it, then switch the signer to the new private key, and only then remove the retired public key from the endpoint. Each key must carry a distinct key id so verifiers can select the right one from the header.\n\nCadence. Rotate signing keys every 90 days, and immediately on suspected compromise.\n\nEmergency rotation. For a compromised key, revoke first and accept the verification failures; publish an incident notice with the affected window.\n\nAutomation. The provided CLI performs the ordered steps and refuses to remove a key that is still within its overlap window."}]}
